A custom loading spinner is a small piece of work, and most of its advantage sits in one place. The stock spinners people reach for most of the time are usually bad. They are often drawn with a border, and a border has to describe a round shape with squares of colour. At the size spinners are drawn at, that looks like a blurred ring with a grey middle. A custom spinner drawn as a single outline, with the corners rounded, has no such compromise. The shape is exactly what it is meant to be.

Then there is the weight. A spinner drawn with plain CSS is a few hundred bytes. An icon pulled from a library is a separate request, and it may come back as a PNG at three different sizes for three different screens. On a slow connection the spinner sometimes arrives before the page does.

The other advantages are about what the user feels, and they are worth taking seriously because most people never notice a good one. A spinner is the thing a person looks at while waiting. If it matches the rest of the interface, the wait feels like part of the product. If it looks like a default template, every few seconds of it remind the person that they are using something that was put together quickly. Your own brand is doing the work in that gap, the logo colour, the type, the rounded corners you use everywhere else.

It also does more than you might expect, because a spinner is one of the few places where an interface can tell a person something true. While it spins, the system is working. When it stops, the work is done. Nobody has to guess. That is worth more than it sounds like, because most people who wait on a webpage have no idea whether it is stuck or only slow.

If the application has a clear main action, such as sending a message or completing a purchase, the spinner can sit inside the button itself. The button keeps its colour and its role, and nothing on the screen moves while the person waits for their answer.

Now the case for leaving it alone. The people who study how users behave disagree more than you would expect about spinners, and I lean toward one side of it with moderate confidence. The case for the plain one is that a spinner tells a person something is loading, and a progress bar tells them how far along they are. Nobody can check either of those claims. A spinner on a task that may take several seconds gives the person no true information at all, and it can leave them staring at it for a minute. Informed designers would also put the two side by side. When someone has asked for something, such as a search, a file upload or a form they typed into, show them a progress bar or a percentage instead. Show the spinner only for small tasks that nobody asked for specially. And if a task takes more than about two seconds, tell them so, with the reason, because a long wait that nobody explains is the worst version of either.

For most of a website's pages, a stock spinner is fine. Nobody is harmed by a grey ring on a blog's sidebar. Custom is worth it on the screens that matter, the login, the checkout, the dashboard.

Here is the base style, with a class of your choice:

```html
<span class="spinner"></span>
```

```css
.spinner {
  display: inline-block;
  box-sizing: border-box;
  border: 3px solid transparent;
  border-top-color: #646cff;
  border-radius: 50%;
  animation: spin 240ms linear infinite;
}

@keyframes spin {
  from { transform: rotate(0deg); }
  to   { transform: rotate(360deg); }
}
```

Change the colour to match your brand. Change `240ms` to make it faster or slower. For a button that submits, add `pointer-events: none` to the button while it is loading, so nobody can submit twice while it spins.

One thing would change the answer. Is this for a site people browse or for a tool people work in? Most of what I said above holds for both, but I am fairly sure of it only for the site. A tool is a different case. When a person is working and their own application freezes, they want to know it, and the honest thing is a message saying what went wrong and what they can do. A spinner that keeps turning on a frozen app only tells them to wait, and waiting is the one thing they cannot see anyone doing. So tell me which kind you are building, and I can be more exact.
